The problem — dimensions change over time.

A fact, once written, is history — a sale that happened is done. But dimensions keep changing. Ana moves from Madrid to Barcelona. A product is re-priced, or shifts category. A customer flips from B-two-C to B-two-B. And these changes are slow — occasional and unpredictable, not a steady stream — which is exactly why they're tricky.

Here's the question that has no default answer. Suppose Ana bought a headset last year, while she was living in Madrid, and today she lives in Barcelona. Now you run "sales by region". Which region should that old sale count toward? You could say Barcelona — where she lives now, reporting the world as it is. Or you could say Madrid — where she lived when the sale actually happened, reporting the world as it was. And here's the thing: both are correct. They answer different questions. "How many customers are currently in each region" wants now. "Revenue by region in twenty twenty-five" wants then. A warehouse has to be able to serve whichever the business needs — and that's a modeling decision, made before the change ever arrives.

Slowly changing dimensions — SCD — is the framework for that decision: for managing and tracking changes to dimension data over time. The different strategies are the SCD types, and the one axis that separates them is this: how much history you keep. Keep none — overwrite, and the past is rewritten; that's Type 1. Keep all — add a new row per change, so every fact stays linked to the version that was true at its time; that's Type 2. Or keep some — a previous-value column, a hybrid, or a side table; Types 3, 4, and 6.

And all of this rides on the surrogate key from module four. Because the fact points at a surrogate, not the natural key, a dimension can hold several versions of the same business entity — same customer i-d, different customer keys — and each fact row points at the one that was current when it happened. So: dimensions change slowly, "what was true then versus now" has no universal answer, and SCD is how you decide, case by case, how much history to keep. This module walks every type.
